<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Complexity rises at emergence and then stops rising", finding_path("complexity-keeps-rising") %>
  on every substrate the programme has tested: an instruction cost, a structured world, a
  tape free to lengthen, and <%= link_to "a contested energy stock", finding_path("complexity-under-contest") %>. Every bet so far changed what a tape can hold or spend. None
  changed what a tape <em>is</em> to its partner: under the default interaction the two
  tapes are concatenated and the program that runs is the pair, so neither tape is the
  other's environment for longer than one interaction.
</p>
<p>
  Sweep 10 changes that. Under <span class="mono">interaction = host</span> the instruction
  pointer ranges over the first tape's bytes only, while both heads still range over the
  whole pair: the second tape never runs a byte of its own and is pure read/write
  substrate. A tape's fate then depends on how the tapes that host it treat it as data,
  which is a pressure on what a tape <em>looks like</em> and not only on what it does — the
  asymmetry every host–parasite system in the literature rests on. The question this page
  will answer: <strong>does complexity keep rising when only one partner's code runs?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The sweep crosses the interaction mode — <span class="mono">concat</span>, the
  whole-concatenation interaction every earlier sweep ran, as the control, against
  <span class="mono">host</span> — with the two room-to-grow caps whose plateau sweep 8
  measured, <span class="mono">max_tape_len</span> 128 and 256, since an asymmetry is only
  readable where the dominant tape has room to get more complicated. Every arm runs 90
  seeds, the control included, at 128×128 for 20 000 epochs at the emergent mutation rate,
  from a 64-byte initial tape.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong> (DESIGN §1.3 sweep 10) and identical to
  sweep 9's, taken on emerged runs only — a crossing the replicator census or the copy rate
  confirmed, not every crossing the detector flagged. Per run, the median of the last decile
  of the post-crossing samples against the median of the first decile. A run counts as
  <em>measured</em> where the first decile of <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span>
  medians above zero, so there is a ratio to take; a run carrying no
  <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</span> sample at all over that span is unmeasured,
  since the core clause cannot be read on it. That measured rule is a
  <strong>post-hoc amendment</strong> (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>,
  2026-09-21), made after sweep 9's data were seen: as registered, the rule also wanted a
  nonzero first-decile median of the conserved core, and the Result below states what that
  rule read beside every reading taken under the amended one. A measured run
  <em>keeps rising</em> when <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> rises by
  at least 20% and <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</span> does not fall in bytes over
  the same span, so a core at zero at both ends has not fallen; it
  <em>plateaus</em> when the last decile sits within ±10% of the first. An arm keeps rising
  where at least half its measured runs do and plateaus where at least half plateau; an arm
  clearing both bars reads <em>mixed</em>, never rising, and one clearing neither, its runs
  having mostly fallen, reads <em>neither</em>. An arm reads at all on two measured emerged
  runs, or on a blank block of ten, which reads as an arm holding no replicator to read
  rather than as an arm still to be tested.
  <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span> is printed beside them and decides
  nothing: it saturates at the cap plus zlib's 11-byte envelope.
</p>
<p>
  The <strong>secondary reading</strong> is on
  <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>: a <span class="mono">host</span> arm whose
  last decile holds more lineages than the <span class="mono">concat</span> control at the
  same cap is an arms race preventing fixation, which is the mechanism an asymmetry is
  supposed to supply. It supports the primary reading and never stands in for it.
</p>

<% reading = @show.complexity_under_asymmetry %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading.any? %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_verdict", reading: reading %>
  <p>
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored runs at render time, and the
    per-arm reading is the one
    <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("asymmetric-execution") %> and its
    transition report draw.
    <% if reading.every_treated_barren? %>
      A blank block of ten reads as an arm holding no replicator (DESIGN §1.3,
      <span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span> 2026-09-15); here the blank block is
      every run of every <span class="mono">host</span> arm.
      <% if reading.barren_beside_emerged_controls? %>
        Running only the first tape's code did not lower the plateau: beside controls that
        held replicators it kept replication from arising at all, which is the mode's own
        null named under <a href="#refutation">What would refute it</a>.
      <% end %>
      So the hypothesis is <strong>not refuted by a plateau</strong> and not supported either — the
      pre-registered test needs a <span class="mono">host</span> plateau to set beside a
      <span class="mono">concat</span> one, and there is none.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_emergence", reading: reading %>
  <% if (pooled = reading.pooled_emergence_sentence) %>
    <p>
      <strong>Pooled over caps, descriptive only.</strong> <%= pooled %>
      This pooling is not part of the pre-registered reading, which sets each arm against
      the control at its own cap.
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_theft", reading: reading %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_reading", reading: reading %>

  <h3>The concat controls, as reference readings</h3>
  <p><strong><%= reading.control_readings_sentence %></strong></p>
  <% if reading.controls.any? %>
    <p>
      The <span class="mono">concat</span> controls read as the reference for these worlds,
      with the pre-registered rule's result above them. They are, seed for seed, the worlds
      <%= link_to "sweep 9's economy-off control", finding_path("complexity-under-contest") %>
      ran: both sweeps ran the default substrate as their control at the same caps, rate,
      size and seeds, and the engine is deterministic in its parameters and seed, so the
      same seeds emerged at the same epochs. They are separate runs in the database, but the
      two findings' controls are one control read twice, not independent evidence.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <h3>The secondary reading</h3>
  <p><strong><%= reading.lineages_sentence %></strong></p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("asymmetric-execution") %> has
    reported a sample in this database, so there is no arm to read here. This page reads the
    arms at render time and states the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2 id="refutation">What would refute it</h2>
<p>
  An asymmetric interaction is the programme's bet that what complexity was missing is a
  partner worth reading, not room or energy. It is refuted if the
  <span class="mono">host</span> arms plateau where their own <span class="mono">concat</span>
  controls plateau — same caps, same rate, same world — which would say that being hosted
  buys this substrate no structure. The mode carries its own null too: if nothing emerges at
  all under <span class="mono">host</span> across a full block of seeds, the arms say that
  halving a pair's code stops abiogenesis, not that asymmetry fails to raise the plateau.
</p>
<% if reading.any? %>
  <p><strong><%= reading.refutation_sentence %></strong></p>
<% end %>
